  "new-year-wishes-spanish": {
  "intro": "Spanish New Year Wishes Generator writes a complete greeting — salutation, wish and sign-off — for the relationship and tone you pick. Spanish marks respect and number in the pronoun, the object clitic and the verb ending all at once, so \"te deseo\", \"le deseo\", \"os deseo\" and \"les deseo\" are four different sentences rather than one with a word swapped; every wording here is written out separately for tú, usted, vosotros and ustedes. Each message comes with a syllable-by-syllable pronunciation guide with the stress marked, a plain English meaning so you can check what you are sending, and the number of SMS parts it needs.",
  "useCases": [
    "Write to a client or an older relative in the usted form without slipping into tú halfway through the sentence.",
    "Send the same New Year wish to a group in Spain (vosotros) and in Latin America (ustedes) with the verb endings correct in both.",
    "Get a pronunciation guide with the stressed syllable marked, so you can read the greeting aloud on a call.",
    "Check whether an accented Spanish greeting will go out as one SMS or three before you send it."
  ],
  "benefits": [
    [
      "All four address forms written out",
      "tú, usted, vosotros and ustedes each get their own wording, so the clitic, the possessive and the verb agree."
    ],
    [
      "Pronunciation with the stress marked",
      "Each word is syllabified and respelt for an English reader, in Latin American seseo or the distinción of central Spain."
    ],
    [
      "SMS cost shown, accents kept",
      "á í ó ú are missing from the GSM-7 alphabet, so the tool counts the parts and offers a safe version that never touches ñ."
    ]
  ],
  "faqs": [
    [
      "How do you say happy new year in Spanish?",
      "The two standard greetings are ¡Feliz Año Nuevo! (feh-LEES AH-nyoh NWEH-boh), 'Happy New Year', and ¡Próspero Año Nuevo!, 'a prosperous New Year'. On the night of 31 December you say ¡Feliz Nochevieja! instead. The ñ in año is not optional — ano is a different word entirely."
    ],
    [
      "Should I use tú, usted, vosotros or ustedes?",
      "Use tú for friends, family, children and most colleagues; usted for clients, officials, teachers and anyone you have not been invited to tutear. For a group, Spain uses vosotros informally and ustedes respectfully, while every Latin American country uses ustedes for all groups. The choice changes the object pronoun (te, le, os, les), the possessive (tu, su, vuestro) and the verb ending together, which is why each version is written out in full here."
    ],
    [
      "Why does an accented Spanish message cost more than one SMS?",
      "The GSM 03.38 7-bit alphabet includes ñ, é, ü, ¡ and ¿, but not á, í, ó or ú. A single one of those four forces the network to encode the whole message as UCS-2 under 3GPP TS 23.038, dropping a single SMS from 160 characters to 70, and to 67 per part once the message is split. The SMS-safe version removes only those four accents and leaves ñ alone."
    ],
    [
      "Do you write Estimado with a comma or a colon?",
      "A colon. Spanish letter conventions put a colon after the salutation — Estimado señor: or Distinguida clienta: — and the comma used in English is treated as an anglicism. Warmer openings like Querida abuela, take a comma, and Hola, María: takes a comma after Hola and a colon after the name."
    ]
  ]
},
